<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solokha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Solokha is a royal Scythian burial mound of the late 5th or early 4th century BCE near the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Excavated by Nikolai Veselovsky in 1912–1913, it contained an intact royal burial with a famous gold comb crowned by a battle scene of three warriors, made by Greek craftsmen for a Scythian patron. The comb and other finds are in the Hermitage Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solokha.jpg" alt="Solokha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Валерий Дед · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solokha_barrow._V_c._BC_(%D0%A6%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BA%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B0%D0%BD)_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
